PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
- The clinical sequence involves valproate (Drug 1), topiramate (Drug 2), and lamotrigine (Drug 3), culminating in Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis (SJS/TEN) characterized by skin blisters.
- Deliberation centers on two interpretations of the initiating biochemical reaction: pharmacokinetic inhibition of UGT-mediated glucuronidation by valproate versus cytochrome P450-mediated epoxidation/arene oxide formation triggering immune-mediated keratinocyte apoptosis.
- The prevailing consensus identifies glucuronidation (specifically the inhibition of glucuronide conjugation via UGT1A4/2B7) as the defining clinical pharmacological trigger, with oxidative epoxidation representing the downstream bioactivation pathway.
}

